@startuml

<style>
.OS {
    LineThickness 5.0
}
</style>

skinparam sequenceArrowColor Black
skinparam sequenceParticipantBackgroundColor White
skinparam sequenceParticipantBorderColor Black
skinparam sequenceLifeLineBorderColor Black
skinparam sequenceGroupBorderColor DarkGray
skinparam sequenceGroupBodyBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor LightYellow
skinparam NoteBorderColor Gray
skinparam NoteTextAlignment left
skinparam ParticipantFontName Courier
skinparam sequenceFontName Courier
skinparam ArrowFontName Courier

title Server <-> Client Sequence Diagram: Library-Internal Fault Handling

note over client_app, server_app
  Companion to server_client_sequence.puml (happy path) and
  server_client_os_fault_sequence.puml (underlying-layer faults).
  This diagram covers deterministic, library-internal rejections -
  bounded resources and configured limits that the library checks
  itself, before any OS/transport call is attempted. These are not
  OS-layer faults and not user contract misuse (see
  research/backlog.md for the latter, tracked as candidate AoUs).
end note

participant "ClientApp" as client_app
participant "IClientConnection" as client_conn
participant "IPC Mechanism"  as os <<OS>>
participant "IServerConnection" as server_conn
participant "IServer" as server
participant "ServerApp" as server_app

' -----------------------------------------------------------------
== Message Exceeds Configured Size Limit (max_send_size / max_reply_size / max_notify_size) ==
' -----------------------------------------------------------------

client_app -> client_conn : Send(oversized_message)
client_conn -> client_conn : validate against\nServiceProtocolConfig::max_send_size
client_conn --> client_app : score::os::Error\n(no IPC attempted)
note left
  Per i_client_connection.h Send() doc: "definitely fail"
  if the message is too big to transport. Validated before
  any OS call - control measure
  MessagePassing.SendBufferArgumentValidation.
end note

client_app -> client_conn : SendWaitReply(message, reply_buffer)
client_conn -> client_conn : validate reply_buffer size against\nServiceProtocolConfig::max_reply_size
client_conn --> client_app : score::os::Error\n(no IPC attempted)

server_app -> server_conn : Notify(oversized_message)
server_conn -> server_conn : validate against\nServiceProtocolConfig::max_notify_size
server_conn --> server_app : score::os::Error\n(no IPC attempted)

' -----------------------------------------------------------------
== Client-Side Async Send Queue Exhausted (max_queued_sends / max_async_replies) ==
' -----------------------------------------------------------------

client_app -> client_conn : Send(message) [repeated, non-blocking]
client_conn -> client_conn : check pre-allocated\nsend_storage_ pool
client_conn --> client_app : score::os::Error\n(pool exhausted; no IPC attempted)
note left
  Grounds control measure BE_SendQueueExhausted: "use a
  pre-allocated, bounded pool for asynchronous client
  send messages and return an error when the pool is
  exhausted without blocking or silently dropping the
  message." Same mechanism applies to
  SendWithCallback()'s max_async_replies limit.
end note

' -----------------------------------------------------------------
== Server-Side Notify Queue Exhausted (max_queued_notifies) ==
' -----------------------------------------------------------------

server_app -> server_conn : Notify(message) [repeated]
server_conn -> server_conn : check per-connection\nnotify queue (max_queued_notifies)
server_conn --> server_app : score::os::Error\n(queue exhausted; no IPC attempted)
note right
  Unlike the server-side incoming max_queued_sends
  (see server_client_os_fault_sequence.puml, "Server-Side
  Incoming Backpressure Delegated to the OS" - currently
  unimplemented as an internal queue), the per-connection
  Notify queue bound by max_queued_notifies is an
  actual internal, deterministic check today.
end note

' -----------------------------------------------------------------
== Safe Failure of Re-Entrant Calls From Within a Callback ==
' -----------------------------------------------------------------

os -> client_conn : [IPC] NOTIFY message
client_conn -> client_app : notify_callback(message)
activate client_app
client_app -> client_conn : SendWaitReply(message, reply_buffer)\n(called from within notify_callback)
client_conn -> client_conn : detect callback-context\nre-entrancy
client_conn --> client_app : score::os::Error\n(safely fails; no deadlock)
deactivate client_app
note left
  Per client-server.md: "it is not allowed to call any
  blocking operation of the Message Passing library
  during the execution of a Client Connection callback
  ... the library provides the guarantee that such a
  call will safely fail." Non-blocking calls (Send,
  SendWithCallback, Stop) remain allowed from callbacks.
end note

@enduml
